lid formal name.\\n\"\n   179→                    \"\\n\"\n   180→                    \"Formal names must include at least one valid Python identifier character.\"\n   181→                )\n   182→            )\n   183→\n   184→        return True\n   185→\n   186→    def _validate_existing_app_name(self, candidate):\n   187→        \"\"\"Perform internal validation preventing the use of pre-existing app names.\n   188→\n   189→        Invoked by validate_app_name; subclasses may override this behavior.\n   190→        \"\"\"\n   191→        if (self.base_path / candidate).exists():\n   192→            raise ValueError(\n   193→                self.input.textwrap(\n   194→                    f\"A {candidate!r} directory already exists.\\n\"\n   195→                    f\"\\n\"\n   196→                    f\"Select a different name, move to a different parent directory, or \"\n   197→                    f\"delete the existing folder.\"\n   198→                )\n   199→            )\n   200→\n   201→    def validate_app_name(self, candidate):\n   202→        \"\"\"Determine if the app name is valid.\n   203→\n   204→        :param candidate: The candidate name\n   205→        :returns: True. If there are any validation problems, raises ValueError with a\n   206→            diagnostic message.\n   207→        \"\"\"\n   208→        if not is_valid_app_name(candidate):\n   209→            raise ValueError(\n   210→                self.input.textwrap(\n   211→                    f\"{candidate!r} is not a valid app name.\\n\"\n   212→                    \"\\n\"\n   213→                    \"App names must not be reserved keywords such as 'and', 'for' and \"\n   214→                    \"'while'. They must also be PEP508 compliant (i.e., they can only \"\n   215→                    \"include letters, numbers, '-' and '_'; must start with a letter; \"\n   216→                    \"and cannot end with '-' or '_').\"\n   217→                )\n   218→            )\n   219→\n   220→        # Validate if the existing app name\n   221→        self._validate_existing_app_name(candidate)\n   222→\n   223→        return True\n   224→\n   225→    def make_module_name(self, app_name):\n   226→        \"\"\"Construct a valid module name from an app name.\n   227→\n   228→        :param app_name: The app name\n   229→        :returns: The app's module name.\n   230→        \"\"\"\n   231→        return app_name.replace(\"-\", \"_\")\n   232→\n   233→    def validate_bundle(self, candidate):\n   234→        \"\"\"Determine if the bundle identifier is valid.\n   235→\n   236→        :param candidate: The candidate bundle identifier\n   237→        :returns: True. If there are any validation problems, raises ValueError with a\n   238→            diagnostic message.\n   239→        \"\"\"\n   240→        if not is_valid_bundle_identifier(candidate):\n   241→            raise ValueError(\n   242→                self.input.textwrap(\n   243→                    f\"{candidate!r} is not a valid bundle identifier.\\n\"\n   244→                    \"\\n\"\n   245→                    \"The bundle should be a reversed domain name. It must contain at \"\n   246→                    \"least 2 dot-separated sections; each section may only include \"\n   247→                    \"letters, numbers, and hyphens; and each section may not contain any \"\n   248→                    \"reserved words (like 'switch', or 'while').\"\n   249→                )\n   250→            )\n   251→\n   252→        return True\n   253→\n   254→    def make_domain(self, bundle):\n   255→        \"\"\"Construct a candidate domain from a bundle identifier.\n   256→\n   257→        :param bundle: The bundle identifier\n   258→        :returns: The candidate domain\n   259→        \"\"\"\n   260→        return \".\".join(bundle.split(\".\")[::-1])\n   261→\n   262→    def make_author_email(self, author, bundle):\n   263→        \"\"\"Construct a candidate email address from the authors name and the bundle\n   264→        identifier.\n   265→\n   266→        The candidate is based on the assumption that the author's name is in\n   267→        \"first/last\" format, or it a corporate name; the \"first\" part is split off, and\n   268→        prepended to the domain extracted from the bundle.\n   269→\n   270→        It's not a perfect system, but it's better than putting up \"me@example.com\" as a\n   271→        candidate default value.\n   272→\n   273→        :param author: The authors name.\n   274→        :param bundle: The bundle identifier.\n   275→        :returns: The candidate author's name\n   276→        \"\"\"\n   277→        return f\"{author.split(' ')[0].lower()}@{self.make_domain(bundle)}\"\n   278→\n   279→    def validate_email(self, candidate):\n   280→        \"\"\"Determine if the email address is valid.\n   281→\n   282→        :param candidate: The candidate email address\n   283→        :returns: True. If there are any validation problems, raises ValueError with a\n   284→            diagnostic message.\n   285→        \"\"\"\n   286→        if parseaddr(candidate)[1] != candidate:\n   287→            raise ValueError(\"Not a valid email address\")\n   288→        return True\n   289→\n   290→    def make_project_url(self, bundle, app_name):\n   291→        \"\"\"Construct a candidate project URL from the bundle and app name.\n   292→\n   293→        It's not a perfect guess, but it's better than having\n   294→        \"https://example.com\".\n   295→\n   296→        :param bundle: The bundle identifier.\n   297→        :param app_name: The app name.\n   298→        :returns: The candidate project URL\n   299→        \"\"\"\n   300→        return f\"https://{self.make_domain(bundle)}/{app_name}\"\n   301→\n   302→    def prompt_divider(self, title: str = \"\"):\n   303→        \"\"\"Writes a divider with an optional title.\"\"\"\n   304→        title = f\"-- {title} \" if title else \"\"\n   305→        self.input.prompt()\n   306→        self.input.prompt()\n   307→        self.input.prompt(f\"{title}{'-' * (MAX_TEXT_WIDTH - len(title))}\", style=\"bold\")\n   308→\n   309→    def prompt_intro(self, intro: str):\n   310→        \"\"\"Write the introduction for a prompt.\"\"\"\n   311→        self.input.prompt()\n   312→        self.input.prompt(self.input.textwrap(intro))\n   313→        self.input.prompt()\n   314→\n   315→    def validate_user_input(self, validator, answer) -> bool:\n   316→        \"\"\"Validate a user's input is acceptable.\n   317→\n   318→        A warning message is issued if input is enabled. Otherwise, an exception is\n   319→        raised and project creation is aborted.\n   320→        \"\"\"\n   321→        error_msg = f\"Invalid value; {answer}\"\n   322→        try:\n   323→            if validator is None or validator(answer):\n   324→                return True\n   325→        except ValueError as e:\n   326→            error_msg = str(e)\n   327→\n   328→        if not self.input.enabled:\n   329→            raise BriefcaseCommandError(error_msg)\n   330→        self.logger.warning()\n   331→        self.logger.warning(error_msg)\n   332→\n   333→        return False\n   334→\n   335→    def validate_override(self, override_value: str | None, validator=None) -> bool:\n   336→        \"\"\"Validates and returns override value for a project configuration.\"\"\"\n   337→        if override_value is not None:\n   338→            self.input.prompt()\n   339→            self.input.prompt(f\"Using override value {override_value!r}\")\n   340→            if not self.validate_user_input(validator, override_value):\n   341→                return False\n   342→        return True\n   343→\n   344→    def validate_selection_override(\n   345→        self,\n   346→        choices: Iterable[str],\n   347→        override_value: str | None,\n   348→    ) -> bool:\n   349→        \"\"\"Validate a project override value against a list of options.\"\"\"\n   350→     